ROLE: product_realist  VOTE: yellow
REAL_USE_CASE: Mixed-precision serving fleets (4-bit quant + FP16 full) that need to avoid wasted cheap-decode cycles on requests that will escalate anyway, specifically when (a) per-request quant degradation is heavy-tailed (not uniform), (b) pre-decode features (prompt perplexity, domain, arithmetic/long-context markers) predict degradation with AUC ≥0.77, and (c) post-decode confidence is at least as noisy as pre-decode signals (so pre-decode ties or modestly beats confidence-threshold on quality). This is a cost-optimization play, not a quality breakthrough.
OPERATIONAL_SIGNIFICANCE: Microbench-only (L0 simulation). The ≤2.4% quality edge over a tuned confidence-threshold cascade is conditional on noise (CI includes 0 at sigma=0.15) and disappears when confidence is clean. The reframed "real win" is COST—skipping the cheap decode—but L0 provides no FLOPs/$ accounting, no real 4-bit vs full model measurements, and no evidence that the cheap-decode saving outweighs router misprediction (oracle gap 0.023–0.033) in a production fleet where the quant model is already heavily optimized (paged kernels, continuous batching). Heavy-tailed degradation is assumed, not measured.
ADOPTION_BARRIER: [1] Unproven heavy-tailed quant degradation in production—claim dies if uniform (L0 null regime AUC ~0.52). [2] Confidence-threshold cascades (FrugalGPT, RouteLLM, LLM-Blender, Tabi) are published, deployed, and STRONG; pre-decode only ties at low noise, wins modestly only when confidence is noisy. [3] Operational overhead: train/maintain quant-sensitivity predictor, extract pre-decode features at serving time, monitor drift; benefit is a small fraction of cheap-decode cost, which is already the "cheap" path. [4] Narrow novelty: quant-sensitivity-specific routing vs generic difficulty routing—why not just use existing difficulty/confidence gates? [5] L1 validation burden: needs real GPTQ/AWQ 4-bit vs full on same prompts, real per-request degradation distribution, real AUC, real confidence baseline, real $ cost model.
RATIONALE: EXP-0020 L0 verdict is PARTIAL (conditionally held). Results/pareto.csv shows router−conf mean Δ +0.0009 to +0.0096 at sigma=0.3 (≤0.96% quality), rising to +0.016–0.024 at sigma=0.6 (≤2.4%). Bootstrap CI at b=0.2 includes 0 at sigma=0.15 (results/bootstrap_router_vs_conf.csv). The packet explicitly reframes the win as COST not quality, and lists L1 gaps: "REAL per-request degradation... Is it actually heavy-tailed? (The claim DIES if real degradation is ~uniform.)" Prior-art caveat names RouteLLM, FrugalGPT, LLM-Blender, Tabi, hybrid-precision serving as published routing baselines. The honest claim is "Pareto-competitive... avoids the cheap decode entirely — but its QUALITY edge... is small and only appears when post-decode confidence is noisy" (packet). Without L1 measurements, operational significance is speculative; the negative result (uniform degradation → no routing benefit) is actionable guidance to skip this path.
